10.2  Keeping the clock right: NTP

TL;DR
  - Computer clocks drift, by seconds a day. NTP asks time servers and corrects
    the clock continuously, to within milliseconds over the Internet.
  - Linux distros run one NTP client: chronyd (Fedora, RHEL) or
    systemd-timesyncd (Debian, Ubuntu, Arch).
  - A wrong clock breaks things far from time: TLS certificates, two-factor
    codes, Kerberos, builds, distributed databases.

      timedatectl                       # "System clock synchronized: yes"
      timedatectl timesync-status       # with systemd-timesyncd
      chronyc tracking                  # with chrony: offset and drift

On your machine
  Zone      Europe/Paris
  RTC       the hardware clock keeps UTC
  Source    tsc (also available: hpet, acpi_pm)
  Sync      systemd-timesyncd, synchronised
  Booted    2026-10-11 06:26 UTC

Pitfalls
  - "Certificate is not yet valid" or "expired" on every site: check the date
    first (11.3).
  - Two NTP clients at once fight over the clock: run one.
  - make: Clock skew detected: files from a machine (or a shared folder) whose
    clock is ahead.
  - In containers, the clock is the host's: fix time on the host.

Deep dive

  ▾ How does NTP measure across a slow network?

    The client notes when it sends a request; the server stamps when it received
    it and when it replied; the client notes when the reply arrives. From the
    four times it computes both the round trip and the offset, assuming the trip
    takes as long each way. Repeating this with several servers filters out the
    outliers and estimates the drift.

  ▾ When do milliseconds not suffice?

    Trading, telecoms and some databases need microseconds. PTP (Precision Time
    Protocol) gets there on a local network, with network cards that timestamp
    packets in hardware. Clouds offer similar precision through their
    hypervisors.

Related
  Commands  timedatectl, chronyc
  Paths     /etc/chrony.conf, /etc/systemd/timesyncd.conf
  See also  idk 10.1 Wall clock and monotonic clock: measuring time right
  See also  idk 11.3 Certificates: how HTTPS proves who it is
